A||N||3||fropuff||Radiator Removal||||1||
Z||000000||fropuff||03-05-2001||10:02 PM||||Hey, I'm getting my radiator "boiled out" so it will get the crap out of it.  I am wondering how to remove it.  I have already drained the coolant out of the system; all I have to do is disconnect it, however it's done.  How do I do this?  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000001||V8Fiero||03-05-2001||10:44 PM||||Heres an abreviated version of the Haynes method:<P>1) Disconnect negative battery cable<BR>2) Drain coolant out of radiator<BR>3) Remove hood. Be sure to scribe marks on the brackets to get it aligned when you put it back on.<BR>4) Disconnect the overflow hose at the filler neck, remove the coolant recovery tank.<BR>5) Remove the fan frame assembly<BR>6) Loosen and disconnect the inlet and outlet hoses from the radiator<BR>7) On automatic models disconnect the transmission coolant lines on the right side of the radiator<BR>8) Remove the bolts attaching the radiator to the radiator support brackets.<BR>9) Cut off the heads of the platic pop fasteners that anchor the air shroud on the top of the radiator. These will have to be replaced. <BR>10) Pull the radiator up from the top.<P><BR>Hope this helps. <P>Scott <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Heisenhund||03-06-2001||12:31 AM||||I just finished this job & the Haynes manual adds a couple extra steps.<P>Fer instance, you don't need to pull the hood off. Definitely a time saver there.<P>All told, this job (including the flush of the cooling system prior to removal) took about 2½ hours.<P>Of course, it didn't solve my overheating problem, but it did eliminate the radiator as the culprit.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||03-06-2001||10:46 PM||||Thanks a lot for the help.  I did not take the hood off to get this done; glad I didn't.  I had a he** of a time getting the one bolt for the recovery tank off though.  The Pontiac geniuses decided to make it angled right by the headlight motor.  I really appreciated that.  Oh well.  The rest was pretty straight-forward.  Thanks again for the help.  My dad's going to bring it to the radiator place tomorrow to get it boiled out.  Thanks!!!||||reg||1
